## Introduction

A successful POP ceiling is 70% preparation and 30% casting. By the time the first mix goes up, the levels have been transferred, the profiles fixed, the scaffolding set, and the failure modes already avoided. Most of the sagging, cracking and uneven-line ceilings you see in Nigerian homes trace back to skipped prep, not bad cement.

This guide covers the full installation sequence from bare soffit to painted finish: surface preparation, level setting, mixing ratios that matter, casting technique, curing before load, cornice fitting and the quality checks between stages. Whether you install yourself or supervise a contractor, knowing this sequence is your quality control.

## First, a Decision — Cast POP, Board POP, or the Hybrid

The trade's two systems (and most homes' honest answer, the hybrid):

**Cast POP** — plaster of Paris mixed and applied in situ: the traditional Nigerian craft, from the hand-run cornices to full cast ceilings. Its strengths: seamless surfaces, unlimited profile freedom, and the repairability of a monolithic skin. Its demands: a skilled hand (the finish is made on the ceiling, by wrist), controlled curing (gypsum sets fast and shrinks), and dry conditions (see [the POP problems guide](/learn/common-pop-ceiling-problems-and-solutions) for the moisture story).

**Board POP** — factory sheets (Gyproc-type gypsum boards on suspended framing): the modern system, dry-jointed and taped. Its strengths: flat, fast, and true; level guaranteed by framing; the electrician-friendly void for wiring. Its demands: the joint and screw discipline (the finish lives in the joints — [the board installation guide](/learn/step-by-step-pop-ceiling-board-installation) covers the system in full) and a dry roof above it.

**The hybrid** — boards for the planes, cast cornices for the edges — is most Nigerian homes' best answer: factory-flat fields, craft-made profiles, and each system doing what it does best. This guide covers the sequence for both, flagging where they diverge.

## Stage 1 — Read the Soffit (the Ceiling's Verdicts)

The concrete soffit above is a substrate like any wall ([the wall preparation logic](/learn/how-to-prepare-walls-for-screeding) applies overhead), with three POP-specific verdicts:

- **Soundness:** the hammer-tap across the slab — hollow, drummy patches of old plaster or defective concrete are future ceilings falling, and they are cut back and repaired *before* the POP goes up (a ceiling repair is scaffolding, dust, and re-painting the room: prep on the ground floor, failure in the bedroom).
- **Level and line:** the measurement that decides the design's honesty — the existing soffit's deviations mapped (string lines and level), because the POP's straight lines are *transferred* from a reading of what is there. The drop depth (typically 100–300 mm for tray designs) is set by the design against the room's height, and the room's usable height is measured before the design that eats it is chosen.
- **The roof's weather-tightness:** the verdict most skipped and most decisive — a POP ceiling under a roof that leaks is a scheduled collapse. Any roof doubt is resolved at [the roofing stage](/learn/choosing-the-right-roof-type-guide), not discovered by the first rainy-season stain blooming through fresh cornices.

## Stage 2 — Setting Out and Level Transfer (Where Straight Ceilings Are Made)

The POP ceiling's geometry is decided on the walls before any material is mixed:

- **The datum:** one true horizontal line transferred around the room at cornice height (laser, water level, or the professional's level-and-string method) — every line the ceiling will have hangs from this one, and a datum that errs puts its error into every profile in the room.
- **The drop lines:** the tray or border depths marked at the walls; the design's levels (per [the designs guide](/learn/types-of-pop-ceiling-designs-and-patterns)) set out on the surfaces that will carry them.
- **The framing or grounds:** for board systems, the suspended grid set to line (hangers to the slab, channels levelled to string — the ceiling's flatness is the framing's flatness, and the boards merely report it); for cast systems, the grounds and running rules fixed true.
- **The wiring routes:** the ceiling's void is the lighting plan's home (see [the lighting integration guide](/learn/pop-ceiling-lighting-integration-guide)) — conduit, boxes, and drops placed *now*, because POP after wiring is surgery, and wiring after POP is demolition.

## Stage 3 — Mixing and Casting (the Craft Half)

For cast POP, the mixing discipline is short and absolute: **plaster into water, never water into plaster** (lumps are born in the wrong order); **fresh, cold-ish water** per batch (warm water accelerates the set — a Nigerian site's afternoon batches set in the bucket); **small batches** (POP's working window is minutes, and the mixer's ambition is the ceiling's enemy); and **no retempering** — a stiffening batch gets faster trowelling, never more water (gypsum's set is chemical, not evaporative; "reviving" it makes weak cast).

The ratios that matter: cast work per the product's own water demand (POP is gauged by consistency — a mix that holds a ridge briefly and beds under the trowel — not by the cement-site's pan-counting habit; gypsum is a different chemistry from cement and inherits none of its forgiving margins), with fibres and binders per [the quantities guide](/learn/how-to-calculate-pop-ceiling-material-quantities) where the design calls for them.

The casting itself: run onto dampened grounds, built in confident passes, ruled to the lines, and closed tight — POP rewards the practiced wrist and punishes the hesitant one, which is why the trade's apprenticeship is long and the good installers are worth their rate.

## Stage 4 — Curing, Drying, and the Load Rule

Gypsum's chemistry sets fast but dries slow, and the two are confused at cost:

- **Set** is minutes to hours — the cast hardening. Working after set is carving, not casting.
- **Drying** is days to weeks — the water leaving (ventilated, weather-permitting; the painted finish waits for the dry, exactly as [the screeding cure/dry logic](/learn/curing-drying-times-screeded-walls) teaches, with gypsum's faster set and comparable drying calendar).
- **The load rule:** nobody stores materials on the new ceiling, hangs from it, or walks the cove lighting over it until it is dry — the sagging ceilings of Nigeria's mid-2000s boom were mostly this: design loaded before chemistry finished.

## Stage 5 — Cornices, Joints, and the Finish Line

The perimeter cornices (the design's frame — [the designs guide](/learn/types-of-pop-ceiling-designs-and-patterns)) are cast or fixed to the set lines, mitred at the corners (the craft's proving ground — a sharp external mitre is the signature of a hand worth its rate), and the joints of both systems are finished: cast work's joints rubbed and fused while green; board work taped, skimmed, and sanded ([the board guide](/learn/step-by-step-pop-ceiling-board-installation) details the jointing protocol).

Then the acceptance checks before paint: the **sight-line** (crouch at one wall, sight the ceiling's lines — waves and dips hide from below and confess from the side), the **torch pass** (raking light on the surface — joint ridges, trowel shadows, pinholes), the **level and line re-check** (the datum honest around the room), and the **dry test** (a polythene square or the touch-and-tape moisture check — [the finishing standards](/learn/finishing-quality-quality-standards) hold the full protocol). Paint follows the checks, never the calendar alone.

## Supervising the Sequence — the Owner's Six Gates

For the supervising owner, the whole guide compresses to six yes/no gates, dated in the site book: soffit repaired and roof proven; datum set and lines true; wiring placed before plaster closed; batches disciplined and cast to line; drying respected before load; sight-line, torch, and dry-test passed before paint. Money follows gates ([the costs guide](/learn/pop-ceiling-cost-estimation-guide) structures the payments) — and the ceiling that passes all six is the one that is still straight a decade on.


## Where the Two Systems Diverge — the Fork Points

Cast and board share the verdicts, the datum, and the wiring-first rule — then diverge at four forks worth naming for the supervisor:

**Substrate logic.** Board systems hang from the slab on a framed grid (the soffit's role is hanger anchors, and its soundness matters at the anchor points); cast systems bond *to* the surfaces (the soffit and walls are the substrate, and their preparation is the bond — the wall-prep logic, overhead). A board ceiling forgives a rough soffit; a cast ceiling needs it right.

**Failure direction.** Board fails at joints and screws (visible lines, screw pops — [the board guide](/learn/step-by-step-pop-ceiling-board-installation) covers the joint discipline); cast fails at bond and shrinkage (sagging runs, cracked mitres — [the problems guide](/learn/common-pop-ceiling-problems-and-solutions) covers the repair side). The supervision attention follows the system: joint inspection on one, bond and cure discipline on the other.

**Moisture tolerance.** Neither family forgives a leaking roof, but board shows water fastest (staining, board softening) while cast hides it longest (and then fails largest). The roof verdict at Stage 1 is not preferential — it is the whole game for both.

**The tradesman's wrist.** Board work is system discipline (grid, screws, tape, compound — learnable); cast work is craft (the profile exists only as well as the hand that ran it). The hybrid's popularity in Nigerian residential work is exactly this economics: factory flatness where flatness matters, hand-craft where craft reads.

## The Sequencing Failures — What the Sagging Ceilings All Share

The failed ceilings of the boom years shared a recipe, and it is a *sequence*, not a material: wiring routed after closing (electricians chiselling fresh cornices for the downlighters the plan forgot), heavy cove lighting hung before drying completed, paint applied over damp plaster (the blistering cycle [the curing guide](/learn/curing-drying-times-screeded-walls) explains), and finish work rushed against handover dates (the torch-pass checks skipped, and every joint ridge published at the house-warming party's evening lights). None of these used bad materials; all of them used good materials out of order — which is the final argument for the six gates: the sequence *is* the quality system, and the gates are its checkpoints.

## Closing

POP installation is sequence: verdicts on the soffit and the roof above it, a datum line that makes every line after it honest, wiring before closing, small disciplined batches cast by a practiced wrist, a drying calendar respected before load, and cornices and joints finished to a sight-line and torch standard. Cast craft, board systems, or the hybrid — the materials are ordinary and the sequence is everything, which is why the good ceilings and the failing ones are made of the same bags, by different orders of work.
